  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThere haven't been many significant changes to the forecast in the \npast 24 hours. Mid-level water vapor places a high over the Rockies \nwith a shortwave trough over Nevada, and weak riding building in \nbehind the trough over California. Offshore flow was observed this \nmorning through typical windy passes, but remains light elsewhere \nat or below 15 mph. Still, the drier air has already begun to \npush to the coastline, evident by the scattering out of low clouds\nearly this morning pre-sunrise. Various weather stations in the \nInland Empire show humidity at 10AM around 26-32%, much less than \nthe 55-70% observed at the same time yesterday. Down into San \nDiego County and even inland Orange County, RH is around 45% \nwhereas yesterday at the same time, values were closer to 65-70%. \n\nTemperatures warm a bit today under the weak upper level ridging and \nclear skies, around 5 degrees above Wednesday's highs. Offshore flow \nwill continue to increase into Saturday as robust high pressure \ndevelops and a low deepens over Arizona. With substantial \ninfluence from the high and warm downsloping winds, temperatures \nwill reach the low to mid 80s for nearly every location west of \nthe mountains, around 10 degrees above normal. The Santa Ana winds\nwill be strongest from Friday afternoon through Saturday \nafternoon, ushering in low humidity around 15-25% for all \nlocations west of the mountains. Despite the drier weather, only \nweak to locally moderate Santa Ana winds are expected (CW3E) with \nthe upper level pattern not as conducive for a stronger event. \nWinds may gust as high as 25-30 mph for foothills and Inland \nEmpire, with up to 40-45 mph for wind-prone passes. With recent \nrainfall and currently sitting at above-normal precipitation for \nthe water year for SoCal, there aren't major fire weather concerns\nwith this event. Still, make sure to do your part and avoid \nactivities that could cause a spark this weekend. \n\nSunday looks to be the hottest day as the high elongates into SoCal \nand offshore winds prevail, though weakening significantly Sunday \nafternoon. Widespread minor to locally moderate HeatRisk will exist \nfor all areas west of the mountains on Sunday as highs hit upper \n80s, around 10-20 degrees above normal. While the afternoons will be \nhot, moderate overnight lows will help offset the heat, limiting \nHeatRisk. \n\nBy Monday, an approaching shortwave trough will help squish the \nhigh a bit and a cooling trend will begin. Zonal flow settles over\nthe area and highs drop 2-3 degrees day over day through Tuesday.\nLatest model guidance shows the high attempting to take hold \nagain mid week, spreading into the Four Corners region which \ncould help keep temperatures above-normal through Wednesday. \nRegardless, the next system in the form of a massive trough is \nexpected to swing into the region Thursday/Friday. Surprisingly, \nglobal model guidance is decently aligned on this next system and\nthe potential for a split off low to form Friday, surging down \ninto Baja. Should this occur, some substantial precipitation would\nbe possible for SoCal, but confidence at this point remains low. \nFor now, the GFS remains the wetter model while the ECMWF attempts\nto provide precipitation with more tempered expectations.",
